    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d882">

                <pb facs="0881" n="869" sameAs="frus:frus1937v01/pg869"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.04418/254</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chairman of the Senate
                        Committee on Foreign Relations</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Pittman</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-04-20">April 20, 1937</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Senator Pittman</hi>: I acknowledge the
                    receipt of your letter of April 10, 1937,<anchor n="3" xml:id="d882-f1"/>
                    transmitting to me for consideration and report the resolution—S. Res.
                        100<anchor n="4" xml:id="d882-f2"/>—requesting that I advise the Senate in
                    regard to the applicability of existing neutrality legislation to certain
                    hypothetical circumstances.</p>
                <p>In reply, I have to inform you that I am entirely in accord with your opinion of
                    this bill which you so ably set forth in your speech in the Senate on April
                        9.<anchor n="5" xml:id="d882-f3"/></p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Cordell Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
